																		Organise the Anomalous Items Log
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wizzblizz" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6760712); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6760712&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608130594" alt="WizzBlizz" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6760712)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wizzblizz" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6760712); return false;" >WizzBlizz</a></span> <span class="odate time_1601533940 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Oct 2020 06:32</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4785898">
									

<p>I propose that entries in the <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/log-of-extranormal-events">Log of Extranormal Events</a>, <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/log-of-anomalous-items">Log Of Anomalous Items</a>, and <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/log-of-unexplained-locations">Log of Unexplained Locations</a> be given some level of organization (similar to the <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/experiment-log-914-hub">914 Experiment Log</a>), primarily so entries in these pages can be referred to elsewhere on the wiki.</p>
<p>For example, consider the following entry in the Log of Extranormal Events:</p>
<div class="blockquote">
<p><strong>Event Description:</strong> A small canoe in the Mississippi River was consumed whole by a Carcharocles megalodon, along with its two occupants. Carcharocles megalodon has been extinct for 2.6 million years, and the river in question is much too shallow to contain a creature of that size.<br />
<strong>Date of Occurrence:</strong> 02/14/2018<br />
<strong>Location:</strong> Undisclosed location on the Mississippi River, USA.<br />
Follow-up Actions Taken: Amnestics were administered to witnesses, and a cover story was circulated claiming the canoe occupants were intoxicated and capsized their vessel accidentally. A task force was sent to locate and capture the anomalous entity, but all attempts to locate the specimen failed.</p>
</div>
<p>If this interests me, and I want to refer to it in an SCP or tale, it is practically impossible to do so. Of course I can go ahead and do it any way but I cannot refer to say, &quot;Extranormal Event 536&quot; because there is no designation given to each entry, and thus no way for a reader to find the entry I am referring to.</p>
<p>In fact, I am only writing this post because this situation has happened to me more than once (I have had an idea for incorporating an entry from one of these pages into something I'm writing or an idea I'm working on but haven't been able to since there is no way to reference it).</p>
<p>This change would be relatively easy to do and is warranted seeing how this is a collaborative writing project and these pages are featured on the very front page. (I'm aware that entries in the Unexplained Locations Log are given designations but this is a bit pointless seeing how they aren't organized in any meaningful way and are still impossible to find for a reader if they are referenced elsewhere without using ctrl+f)</p>
<p>And while we're at it, maybe split them into some sections with collapsibles.</p>
<p>Edit: Just to clarify in case anyone gets the wrong idea, I'm not asking that entries be given their own anchors or anything like that, just some designation that allows them to be referred to elsewhere on the site.</p>

							</div>

								
