					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/bottleofwater" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3212940); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3212940&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608130594" alt="Bottleofwater" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3212940)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/bottleofwater" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3212940); return false;" >Bottleofwater</a></span> <span class="odate time_1602611744 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Oct 2020 17:55</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4794856">
									

<p>Let me give a bit of my opinion here.<br />
From one point of view, assuming that the log is still being pruned, I guess that instead of organizing the current logs you could just put a requirement of an ID/ event number on the new ones, and eventually the older ones without IDs would get deleted. This would cause a lot of drama with people not reading the requirements and stuff, which brings me to my other POV.</p>
<p>Too much hassle.</p>
<p>Is it really worth it going through the whole thing, which is long as hell, just so a minority of people end up being able to link an article conveniently? You could continue arguing the point, but that'd take more time than just grabbing the log, and either putting it in a collapsible or briefly describing it in a footnote. You could also make an event up.</p>
<p>Either way, I believe that the occurrence of a log from the LOEE, LOAI, or LOUL being linked to an article is so rare that I see no point in taking any action at all.</p>

							</div>

								
					
